# known-failures.allow — the PostgreSQL regression-suite ratchet ledger.
# Companion: scripts/rowsort_compare.py --allow-file. Precedent:
# crates/_support/seams_init/tests/lint-determinism.allow.
#
# One test name per line. A listed test may fail without failing CI; a test
# NOT listed that fails breaks the build, and a listed test that stops failing
# is reported stale and also breaks the build, so this file can only shrink.
# Removing an entry is the unit of progress.
#
# SEED: 2026-09-04, first execution of the vendored suite (submodule pinned to
# REL_18_3 = 62d6c7d3df6287f1bd83199c1a746e50d31571a0) against a pgrust server
# built from main @ 489c1fc542. pg_regress raw: 157/230 "failed", but 136 of
# those were the overlay's own -- pgrust:rowsort marker echo, which this
# comparator strips.
#
# RESEEDED: 2026-09-05 against main @ d3eeb7edb3, after three comparator
# defects were fixed. 230 tests checked, 210 exact, 20 fail, 0 error;
# isolation 131/131. The seed's "17 fail + 1 error" was wrong in three ways,
# and the corrections are the entries below:
#
#   * The comparator's test set was the overlay directory, so only 154 of the
#     230 scheduled tests were classified at all. create_index, tidscan,
#     incremental_sort and memoize were failing the whole time behind a green
#     ratchet. They are now listed.
#   * compression was never failing. pg_regress accepts expected/<name>.out OR
#     any of expected/<name>_0..9.out; compression matches compression_1.out
#     (the no-LZ4 baseline) exactly. Comparing only against compression.out
#     produced a 181-line diff where the right baseline gives none. Removed.
#   * numeric_big never ran. It is overlaid but absent from parallel_schedule
#     (upstream runs it only via EXTRA_TESTS), and "produced no output at all"
#     was the comparator describing a test nobody asked to execute. Removed;
#     it is now reported as NOT CHECKED rather than scored.
#
# Clusters, for whoever picks these up:
#   generated columns   — output stops early (~900 deleted lines, 0 added):
#                         reads as an unported feature or an aborting error,
#                         not a wrong answer.
#   COPY                — copy/copy2/copyselect.
#   partitioning/planner— mixed +/-, consistent with plan-shape divergence from
#                         the redesigned executor. Whoever picks these up will
#                         need to write the adjudication from scratch against
#                         main: is the differing plan shape permitted by the
#                         SQL semantics, or does a row actually change?
#   parallel plan shape — create_index, tidscan, incremental_sort, memoize.
#                         One mechanism: pgrust plans these queries in parallel
#                         (Gather / Workers Planned: 1 / Parallel Seq Scan)
#                         where C's expected output is serial. Every changed
#                         line in all four diffs is inside an EXPLAIN block and
#                         no result row differs, so this is plan shape, not a
#                         wrong answer. Adjudicate against main: EXPLAIN output
#                         is not part of the SQL contract, so the question is
#                         whether any result row differs, and none does here.
#   security            — privileges, rowsecurity. NOT the same defect as
#                         contrib/citext's create_index_acl (fixed d3eeb7edb3):
#                         citext's test confirms DefineIndex uses the ORIGINAL
#                         userid where necessary, privileges' regress_sro_user
#                         tests look for the opposite, that it uses the TABLE
#                         OWNER userid where necessary. Two items, not one.

alter_table
copy
copy2
copyselect
create_index
generated_stored
generated_virtual
incremental_sort
join
limit
memoize
partition_aggregate
partition_join
partition_prune
portals
privileges
rowsecurity
select_distinct
subselect
tidscan
